{"sourceSlug": "jean-parmentier", "sourceName": "Jean Parmentier", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "jacques-cartier", "targetName": "Jacques Cartier", "context": "Jean Parmentier's pioneering long-distance voyages and cartographic contributions likely influenced later French explorers like Jacques Cartier in their own global expeditions."}], "places": [{"name": "Dieppe, France", "role": "Birthplace and Departure Point"}, {"name": "Sumatra, Indonesia", "role": "Destination and Deathplace"}, {"name": "Tiku, Indonesia", "role": "Trading Port"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jean Parmentier", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jean-parmentier"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3175845", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Parmentier_(explorer)"], "tags": ["Explorers & Navigators", "cultural-diffusion", "environmental-history", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Jean Parmentier represents a significant early French attempt to engage in global exploration and trade, directly challenging Iberian monopolies. His 1529 expedition, despite his death, provided vital navigational data and inspired subsequent French maritime ambitions, influencing the nation's eventual colonial expansion.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-02T07:03:03Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30736914673", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jean Parmentier (1494–1529), born in Dieppe, France, was a navigator, cartographer, and poet. Jean and his brother Raoul made numerous voyages for the shipowner Jean Ango.", "newValue": "Jean Parmentier (1494–1529) emerged from Dieppe, France, as a prominent Renaissance navigator, cartographer, and poet whose ambitious 1529 expedition to the East Indies represented a pivotal, albeit ultimately tragic, early French attempt to challenge Portuguese dominance in the lucrative spice trade. His endeavors underscore the burgeoning French maritime spirit of the early 16th century, driven by powerful mercantile figures like shipowner Jean Ango and a growing national desire for direct access to global wealth and prestige, characteristic of the Age of Discovery.

Parmentier's most significant undertaking was his leadership of the 1529 Sumatra expedition, commanding two ships, the *Sacre* and the *Pensée*, under Ango's commission. Departing Dieppe on April 5, 1529, with his brother Raoul and the esteemed cartographer Pierre Crignon, the fleet navigated around the Cape of Good Hope, successfully reaching the western coast of Sumatra. They engaged in trade for valuable spices such as pepper in the port of Tiku, demonstrating the technical feasibility of French long-distance oceanic ventures and gathering crucial intelligence on Asian trade routes.

